+# Local validation notes
+
+This is a local companion to the upstream research prototype, not upstream product documentation. It records a reproducible, credential-free check and the remaining live integration requirements.
+
+## Baseline
+
+- Upstream: <https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent-prototype>
+- Commit: `6337fc823ce20251c8de8085948165c81aaa701e` (2026-10-31).
+- Tested on macOS arm64, Node.js `24.14.1`, pnpm `11.7.0`.
+- Harness runtime: `@deepseek-ai/dsh@0.2.1-rc.3`, installed in the ignored `.artifacts/runtime` directory.
+- The original code has an MIT license; original attribution remains unchanged.
+
+Run the project checks:
+
+```sh
+pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --ignore-scripts
+pnpm check
+```
+
+The initial upstream check passed typechecking and 1196 of 1197 tests. One test matched an English Git identity error, while Git on this machine emitted Chinese. The local patch pins `LC_ALL=C` in that test's child-process environment. The original 1197 tests, typecheck and build then passed. No production authorization or execution behavior was changed.
+
+The additional `tests/cross-channel-replay.spec.ts` runs the real `Engine` and SQLite `Store`, with scripted external boundaries:
+
+```sh
+pnpm exec vitest run tests/cross-channel-replay.spec.ts
+```
+
+It checks an IM request, duplicate intake, a task binding to a GitHub pull-request thread, a subscribed review delivered without another assessment, an actual SQLite close/open, interrupted-assignment recovery, an IM follow-up on the same task, a simulated merge event, and an idempotently queued result addressed to the originating IM thread.
+
+**This is not a live GitHub/Feishu test.** No model is called, no source code is generated, no PR is created or merged, and the reply remains in a local outbox. The assessor and executor ports are scripted. SQLite persistence, domain state transitions, routing, deduplication and receipt behavior use upstream implementation.
+
+Optional evidence export in a POSIX shell:
+
+```sh
+mkdir -p .artifacts
+SUPERAGENT_REPLAY_REPORT=.artifacts/cross-channel-replay.json \
+  pnpm exec vitest run tests/cross-channel-replay.spec.ts
+```
+
+In PowerShell the equivalent environment-variable syntax is:
+
+```powershell
+New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force .artifacts | Out-Null
+$env:SUPERAGENT_REPLAY_REPORT = '.artifacts/cross-channel-replay.json'
+pnpm exec vitest run tests/cross-channel-replay.spec.ts
+Remove-Item Env:SUPERAGENT_REPLAY_REPORT
+```
+
+The replay is intended to be portable; Windows has not been executed here. The upstream service manager itself imports Python `fcntl` and uses `tmux` and POSIX shell commands. Do not infer native Windows service support from the existence of PowerShell worker tools. Use an appropriate Linux/WSL environment if following the upstream deployment scripts; that path is not validated by this local run.
+
+## Start an isolated local panel
+
+The following commands create a **new task-local profile**. Never use `--from-default-profile` to overwrite an existing profile.
+
+```sh
+npm install --prefix .artifacts/runtime --no-save @deepseek-ai/dsh@0.2.1-rc.3
+export DSH_HOME="$PWD/.artifacts/dsh-home"
+node .artifacts/runtime/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh/lib/bin.js \
+  --profile superagent-lab --from-default-profile web --dump-config > /dev/null
+node .artifacts/runtime/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh/lib/bin.js \
+  plugin --profile superagent-lab add "link:$PWD"
+```
+
+Set the new profile's `cordis.patch.yml` using `examples/profile.cordis.patch.yml`, with a task-local absolute `stateDir` and an unused loopback port. Then start:
+
+```sh
+node .artifacts/runtime/node_modules/@deepseek-ai/dsh/lib/bin.js \
+  --profile superagent-lab --no-open
+```
+
+Open the private URL printed by the runtime. Its query token grants local panel access: do not publish it in screenshots or documentation. The task-local profile neither replaces nor updates the user's normal DSH profile.
+
+In this run the real panel loaded at loopback port 3082, including the Superagent overview and settings. Its honest initial state was `setupPending`: the GitHub repository and allowed sender lists were unset. The management API returned the same reason. A loaded panel is not an accepted work item.
+
+## Bounded live GitHub acceptance
+
+After the credential-free replay, the existing DeepSeek account login was recognized by the dedicated Harness instance. A minimal Router request returned the requested reply, then a bounded live task used the profile's default model. No API key or account information was added to tracked files.
+
+The operator's existing GitHub login was used only for this explicit, disposable-repository test. A manually dispatched GitHub Actions workflow supplied a distinct `github-actions[bot]` event identity. The policy listed that bot and allowed only the exact `[SUPERAGENT-LIVE-TEST]` font-task marker in the configured repository, with one worker, limited code/test/README scope and no merge permission in the task instructions. This exercises the supported inferred-candidate path; a bot comment is not converted into human authorization.
+
+Observed live results:
+
+- One bot comment on Issue #1 was polled by the real GitHub Source and accepted into one Ticket.
+- The real Executor prepared a worktree, ran model/tool steps, implemented independent font families, ran tests, and opened draft PR #2.
+- A controlled compatibility comment on that PR reached the same task. Without operator forwarding, the Worker added a second commit documenting the widened return shape and testing the legacy-field values.
+- The same task ID and PR were retained; no second PR was created. The PR stayed draft/open, with no merge or issue closure.
+- Final head `140491003f542db4dd5eed9c80d92bf88f47ad8b` passed 8 local tests and GitHub Actions on Linux, macOS and Windows. An independent read-only review found no required changes in the three changed files.
+- Task processing was paused again after the Worker reported ready for review.
+
+The first PR-comment workflow failed because its token had Issue access only and used the issue-comment command. The fixture workflow was corrected to grant PR access and use `gh pr comment` for PR phases; the successful comment and its run were checked before drawing conclusions about routing.
+
+`ready` is distinct from `blocked`/`failed`: `waitsForPerson` holds automatic inputs after the latter, not every report awaiting review. In-scope automated follow-ups can wake a ready worker; they do not expand its immutable mandate. Do not infer a general bot-input hold from the panel's `person/review` label.
+
+This run did not test live Feishu, merging, live host interruption or arbitrary fault recovery. Those remain separate from the local SQLite/interruption replay.
+
+## Further live acceptance
+
+1. Configure a model provider in the dedicated profile. Keep keys out of tracked files.
+2. Name a disposable GitHub repository and a separate agent GitHub account, with only the needed repository access. Do not reuse the operator's account as an unattended bot.
+3. Configure allowed requesters and an isolated execution environment. Upstream workers run with the OS user's permissions; directory shims are not a sandbox.
+4. Optionally configure a published Feishu bot and a specific test group.
+5. Exercise the full chain: allowed IM request → one task → real code/test output → one PR → GitHub review → updated same PR/task → human merge → concise result back to the original thread.
+6. Interrupt and restart the host during the test, replay one duplicate event, and check external GitHub state before repeating any operation.
+
+The core validation created no accounts or credentials. A disposable fixture repository was subsequently provisioned using the operator's existing GitHub login; it was private during the live test and prepared for public release afterwards. Replace the operator's CLI with a dedicated, restricted agent identity and enforce execution isolation before unattended operation. A trusted one-time test under the operator's OS user is not a production permission boundary.
+
+## Local changes
+
+- `tests/environment.spec.ts`: deterministic language for the diagnostic being asserted.
+- `tests/cross-channel-replay.spec.ts`: a credential-free integration replay for readers.
+- `docs/local-validation.md`: this record and the live acceptance boundary.
+- `docs/reliability-proposal.md`: an unimplemented extension design, separate from verified upstream behavior.
+
+These changes have not been submitted upstream.
