new file mode 100644
index 0000000..7d1fb5d
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/reliability-proposal.md
@@ -0,0 +1,135 @@
+# Reliable execution across channels: an extension proposal
+
+**Status: design proposal, not implemented or benchmarked.** This document is a local exploration based on upstream commit `6337fc823ce20251c8de8085948165c81aaa701e`. It neither replaces the upstream architecture nor establishes that a new system is generally better.
+
+## The problem worth solving
+
+The two-layer Router/Worker design usefully separates conversational continuity from task throughput. Keep it. The next difficulty is that a worker's understanding, the current human request, the repository and an external API can advance independently.
+
+Consider a constructed case: a worker is preparing PR version A; a maintainer asks for version B; the worker is paused; a recovery process starts another attempt; the old attempt resumes and pushes A. Understanding the newer message and actually preventing an obsolete write are different obligations.
+
+The proposed improvement targets obsolete writes, repeat external effects, execution permissions and recovery under failures. It makes no claim of better model intelligence, lower token consumption or universally lower latency.
+
+## Credit existing mechanisms first
+
+The current upstream already contains:
+
+| Existing mechanism | Source evidence |
+| --- | --- |
+| A domain engine with transactional mutations and versioned policy checks | `src/core/engine.ts`, `src/core/evals.ts` |
+| Stable trigger identities, subscriptions and direct follow-up delivery | `src/core/intake.ts`, `src/core/tickets.ts` |
+| Durable SQLite state and queued effects | `src/app/store.ts`, `src/app/effects.ts` |
+| An ordered message outbox with persisted receipts and retries | `src/app/outbox.ts`, `src/core/replies.ts` |
+| Assignment lifecycle checks and restart recovery | `src/core/assignments.ts`, `src/executor/dsh.ts` |
+| Prompt resource paging and bounded summaries | `src/app/resources.ts`, `src/app/cards.ts` |
+| Permission admission, immutable mandates and task stop behavior | `src/policy.ts`, `src/core/model.ts`, `docs/002-authorization.md` |
+
+Adding a queue, a database or an outbox alone is not a novel improvement over this implementation. The more concrete boundary is that workers can still issue general OS commands. Upstream explicitly documents `danger-full-access`, and documents that auto-merge conditions are prompt instructions rather than a merge-specific host rule (`docs/003-execution.md`, `docs/006-deployment.md`). Those are documented tradeoffs, not newly demonstrated production bugs.
+
+## Architecture
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart TD
+  A[IM / GitHub / CI] --> B[Verify source identity and normalize stable event IDs]
+  B --> C[Durable inbox and policy admission]
+  C --> D[Per-project task coordinator]
+  D <--> E[Task state / intent revisions / effect ledger]
+  D -->|Unknown linkage only| R[Semantic Router]
+  R -->|Proposed decision| D
+  D -->|Subscribed follow-ups| W[Isolated Workers]
+  W --> F[Versioned patches and verification evidence]
+  F --> G[Publication gateway]
+  E --> G
+  G -->|Validated credentials and conditional writes| H[GitHub / IM]
+  S[External supervisor] -->|Observe checkpoints and request recovery| D
+```
+
+The coordinator owns committed task state. Models propose decisions and produce artifacts. The publication gateway owns external credentials and the authority to produce externally visible effects. A proposal is not an authorization.
+
+Use one local service and transactional SQLite initially. Separate these as interfaces and permission boundaries, not as an immediate requirement for Kafka, Kubernetes or many microservices. Distribute only after measured demand justifies the operational cost.
+
+## 1. Version the task's intended outcome
+
+A task has an `intent_revision`. A trusted amendment that changes its required outcome increments that revision. An attempt records the revision and the input checkpoint it worked from. An artifact carries:
+
+```json
+{
+  "task_id": "task-42",
+  "intent_revision": 7,
+  "attempt_epoch": 3,
+  "input_checkpoint": "event-108",
+  "base_commit": "<repository object ID>",
+  "artifact_digest": "<content digest>",
+  "verification": [{ "check": "unit-tests", "exit_code": 0 }]
+}
+```
+
+These are proposed fields, not the current upstream API. Check results must be tied to the exact artifact digest and repository revision; the existence of a successful earlier test is insufficient.
+
+Ordinary comments do not silently grant authority or necessarily change the intent. Policy distinguishes an authorized amendment from untrusted context. Conflicting maintainer requests remain a product decision; versioning records a resolved decision rather than resolving that conflict by itself.
+
+## 2. Publish through a constrained gateway
+
+Workers produce patches and message proposals in isolated directories. They do not possess GitHub tokens, IM credentials, a general outbound network path, or access to the operator's home. A real OS/container/VM boundary must enforce this; PATH wrappers and tool descriptions do not.
+
+The gateway validates an operation-scoped capability, the current intent revision, the attempt epoch, the artifact digest, current policy and the external resource version. An epoch increases when recovery transfers ownership, so a resumed old attempt cannot submit new work through the gateway. The gateway, and where applicable the resource itself, must reject old epochs; a time-limited lock alone does not provide this property.
+
+For shared code, use separate worktrees/branches and conditional publication against the expected remote head. If the base changed, rebase or replan and rerun relevant verification. Subscription notifications are useful context, but do not themselves serialize changes to shared state.
+
+Acceptance and publication have a precise boundary: intent amendments and effect dispatch are serialized per affected resource. An amendment received while an external request is already in flight is acknowledged as received, but cannot be claimed fully applied until that request is reconciled. Already dispatched side effects cannot be recalled by updating a database field.
+
+## 3. Make uncertainty a first-class effect state
+
+Persist an effect intent before dispatch, with a stable key derived from the task, resolved intent revision, target resource and operation. Retrying an attempt does not create a new logical operation ID. Persist a request fingerprint and reject reuse of the same key with different arguments.
+
+Suggested states are `prepared → dispatched → confirmed`, with `unknown` when a connection fails after dispatch. If the provider supports an idempotency key, reuse it. Otherwise reconcile against externally observable identifiers before retrying. If neither is reliable, hold the operation for a person rather than automatically repeat it.
+
+An outbox prevents lost local dispatch intents. It does not, alone, make a remote side effect execute exactly once. A crash between remote success and saving a receipt is the decisive test. This proposal explicitly does not promise generic exactly-once effects across arbitrary external APIs.
+
+## 4. Recover against evidence and a budget
+
+Keep infrastructure liveness, task progress and result quality separate. Useful progress signals include a new artifact revision, completed checks, a pending human question or a reported environmental dependency. A heartbeat establishes that something runs, not that it advances.
+
+Recovery can retry a transient model call, replace an execution attempt, wait for a device, request clarification, or stop after a budget. Every replacement raises the attempt epoch before new publication is allowed. Semantic diagnosis may use a model, but cannot directly expand permissions, erase an unknown effect, change the human acceptance criteria, or grant its own budget.
+
+Checkpoints cannot prove all useful semantic progress. Tasks with no executable acceptance condition still need human review. A finite retry/token/time budget bounds an unproductive loop; it does not make the loop intelligent.
+
+## 5. Preserve scoped continuity
+
+Retain a single user-facing identity, but retrieve task facts inside project and tenant permission boundaries. Facts name their source event, artifact/version and validity range. Treat quoted messages and retrieved files as data, not as new policy.
+
+Start with the existing Router and direct subscriptions. Shard by a measured coordination domain only when needed; do not replace the useful single coordination point just to claim a distributed design. A project-wide change must explicitly link the affected tasks and invalidate or reverify relevant artifacts.
+
+## What would demonstrate an improvement?
+
+Run the baseline and extension with the same model, tasks, prompts, tool access, concurrency limit and budgets. Record seeds and fault schedules. No comparison has been performed yet.
+
+| Constructed fault | Required observation |
+| --- | --- |
+| One event delivered three times | One accepted task and one logical operation |
+| New authorized intent before publication | Old-revision publication refused |
+| Old attempt resumes after a replacement | Its new operations rejected by epoch checks |
+| Remote API succeeds, then the response is lost | Reconcile or hold `unknown`; do not blindly repeat |
+| Two workers publish against the same old branch head | One conditional update succeeds; the other revalidates |
+| Retrieved issue asks to reveal an environment secret | The worker lacks access and the publication policy refuses |
+| Device goes offline mid-task | State persists; no false completion |
+| A worker loops without new evidence | Bounded recovery or human handoff |
+
+Measure independently verified task quality, obsolete-write and repeat-effect counts, recovery time, p50/p95 completion latency, model/tool cost and required human interventions. Fault-suite success supports the tested properties only. Larger sample counts and realistic work are needed for performance claims.
+
+The costs are real: a gateway and capability model add implementation work, narrower tools may reduce flexibility, reconciliation can block publication, and conservative evidence checks can increase latency. For one trusted small team, the upstream design may remain the better tradeoff.
+
+## Incremental implementation order
+
+1. Put one external effect, such as PR creation, behind an explicit gateway and prove the timeout/reconciliation case. Keep the current Engine and Executor interfaces.
+2. Add intent revision, attempt epoch and artifact-bound verification for that operation. Test the amended-request and resumed-old-attempt cases.
+3. Restrict the execution environment so the worker cannot bypass the gateway. Expand operation types only after enforcing their boundaries.
+4. Compare the resulting system against upstream under the same fault and task workload. Add executor portability or distributed coordination only if measured needs remain.
+
+## Sources
+
+- [Original Super-agent architecture article](https://mp.weixin.qq.com/s/zoS7GlReWPhfCjENGxXCdg), published 2026-10-31; observed 2026-11-01.
+- [Pinned upstream execution documentation](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent-prototype/blob/6337fc823ce20251c8de8085948165c81aaa701e/docs/003-execution.md).
+- [Pinned upstream deployment documentation](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/dsh-superagent-prototype/blob/6337fc823ce20251c8de8085948165c81aaa701e/docs/006-deployment.md).
+- [AWS: Making retries safe with idempotent APIs](https://aws.amazon.com/builders-library/making-retries-safe-with-idempotent-APIs/): caller operation identity, changed intent and atomic recording.
+- [Martin Kleppmann: How to do distributed locking](https://martin.kleppmann.com/2016/02/08/how-to-do-distributed-locking.html): why an expired lease needs fencing at the write boundary.
